/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed684050904/1/des.md:167:- An L-shaped triomino has exactly one "corner cell" — the pixel that has two orthogonal neighbors within the triomino. The missing cell of the 2x2 box (the empty fourth cell) defines the arrow direction: the triomino "points" from its corner cell toward the missing cell.
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed684050904/1/des.md:171:- For each L-shaped triomino, identify its corner cell and the direction toward the missing cell of its 2x2 bounding box. This is the "arrow direction" (one of four cardinal directions: up, down, left, right).
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed684050904/1/des.md:172:- From the corner cell, cast a ray in the arrow direction. Draw each ray pixel in the triomino's color, continuing until the ray reaches either a grey obstacle pixel or the grid boundary.
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed684050904/1/des.md:174:- The triomino pixels themselves remain unchanged. Rays from different triominoes that cross the same cell: the ray drawn later (triominoes processed top-to-bottom, left-to-right by corner cell position) overwrites the earlier one.
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed684050904/1/des.md:177:- Each L-triomino is a disguised directional arrow. The "missing corner" of the 2x2 bounding box is the key to determining the ray direction — solvers must recognize that the empty cell (negative space of the bounding box) encodes directionality. This is subtle because all four orientations of the L-shape look similar at first glance, and the directional information is in what is absent, not what is present.
